            <ArticleTitle>Simultaneous bilateral ovarian torsion in the follicular phase after gonadotropin stimulation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>462.e13-4</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present and discuss the first report of follicular phase bilateral ovarian torsion following a cancelled IVF cycle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University-based assisted reproductive technology program.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 41-year-old nulligravid patient on day 3 of her menses following a cancelled IVF cycle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Gonadotropin ovulation induction; laparoscopy with detorsion of left and right ovaries; aspiration of cysts.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Ovarian torsion resolved; follicular development in the following natural cycle.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">This is a unique case of simultaneous bilateral ovarian torsion following a cancelled IVF cycle and presenting in the ensuing follicular phase. Physicians should be aware of this unusual risk related to persistently enlarged ovaries in the cycle following gonadotropin stimulation. Furthermore, management of the infertility patient should be conservative and focused on ovarian preservation whenever feasible. Early surgical intervention can permit reperfusion and salvage of the affected adnexa.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
